// @check
// @error A Fd-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// @error A K-keyed Map isn't supported yet
// THE GATE'S REMAINING JOB, pinned beside `newtype-key-annotation.vl`. Resolving a map
// key through its LOWERING rather than its spelling admits a newtype over `i32`/`string`
// — it must not admit a newtype over a base the map rep has no hash for, and it must not
// admit a literal union.
//
// `type Fd = new f64` erases to `f64`, which has no hash (the rep has exactly two,
// `__str_hash__` and the integer mix), so the brand changes nothing: it is refused with
// the same wording its base is, naming the SPELLING the user wrote.
//
// `type K = "a" | "b"` is the deliberate negative, not an oversight. A NAMED string
// literal union reps as an interned i32 ATOM while its map-key spelling stays `K` at
// emit (`numLitUnionBaseName` excludes the string base for exactly that reason), so
// admitting it would leave the key expression lowering as an atom while the map struct
// resolved string-keyed — a silent wrong answer, strictly worse than this reject.
// Admitting it needs the key RENDER and `.keys()`'s element type to move together.
type Fd = new f64
type K = "a" | "b"

const fm: {[Fd]: i32} = Map()
const km: {[K]: i32} = Map()
